branch: wu/export/clear
tests:
  unit:
    count: 17
    covers: "src/store.rs (7 tests + the extended ring_accounting proptest) - remove_matching taking exactly the filtered rows while the others stay listable and gettable; the re-weighing made OBSERVABLE rather than read off total_bytes alone, by following a partial clear with an insert that fits under the corrected total and would have evicted under the stale one (this is the assertion that would catch the byte arithmetic being wrong in a way an equality check on a private field cannot); a default filter clearing a store the cap has already reduced to a single row, which is the one place removal deliberately differs from eviction (evict keeps the last row alive so an oversized flow stays inspectable, and a clear that left a row behind would not be a clear); remove-by-id returning true once then false, and the surviving order still newest-first after a row is taken from the MIDDLE, which is the case eviction never exercises because it only pops the front; the batch event carrying every id that left with exactly one event for the batch and NOTHING at all for an empty selection (a no-op is not news a subscriber redraws for); removing one flow announcing it as a batch of one, and a missing id announcing nothing; and the connection tally surviving both doors, extending the existing note_connection assertion to removal. ring_accounting gained a removals dimension rather than a second proptest beside it, per the epic plan's explicit instruction: inserted flows now carry one of three hosts so a host filter selects a genuine subset, and generated instructions interleave a filtered clear, a remove-by-id of the oldest row, and a remove of an id that was never there, alongside the inserts, imports and evictions the property already drove. The invariant is unchanged - the sum of retained_bytes over what the ring holds equals total_bytes - with one assertion added that list() and the map agree on length, which is what catches an order left holding a dead id. Added by the review round: removing_a_middle_row_leaves_the_order_intact_around_it (the single-id path mends the order by FINDING its row rather than rebuilding, so it must find the right one - a middle row, which a position scan could get wrong at either end) and a_repeated_id_is_debited_once (an id named twice debits once, or total_bytes would fall below what the ring actually holds - the guard that makes the filter-then-debit shape safe). Both were mutation-tested against deliberately broken code and observed to FAIL before being trusted, so neither passes vacuously. The generator was PROVEN to reach the removal path rather than assumed to: a temporary inverted assertion on a removal counter was run and failed with a nonzero count, so removals demonstrably fire and demonstrably remove rows, and the scaffolding was then stripped. src/api/routes.rs (5) - the count and the emptied listing; the filter reaching the store and an empty match answering 200 with zero rather than 404 (settled question 6: the request expresses an end state and that state is already true); an unrecognised source REFUSING the clear and removing nothing, which is the one refusal that matters most on a destructive route because silently acting on a different set than the caller named is unrecoverable; the id route's three answers (200 with the same envelope, 404 for an id the ring does not hold, 400 for one that is not a flow id); and the tally read back through /stats after a clear, so 'empty, not restarted' is asserted at the endpoint a client actually reads. src/api/mod.rs (1) - DELETE answers on /flows and /flows/{id} and does NOT answer on /flows/export or /flows/import, asserted because axum returns 405 either way and a method landing on the wrong path would regress in silence; and the existing preflight test's assertion widened to GET, POST, DELETE, OPTIONS. src/api/view.rs - the FlowsCleared SSE mapping asserted as a whole JSON value (event name cleared, a flow_ids array) rather than field by field, so the wire shape is pinned."
  integration:
    count: 5
    covers: "tests/api.rs over the real axum router, which is this WU's bounded contract - the store's removal reached through the HTTP a client actually speaks. clearing_the_capture_empties_the_listing_and_tells_the_stream subscribes to /stream BEFORE the delete, then asserts the count, the emptied listing, that the frame contains event: cleared, that 'event:' appears exactly once in it (the batch claim, not just the presence of an event), and that /stats reports the same tally after. a_filtered_clear_removes_only_its_matches_and_leaves_the_rest_whole gives the survivor both a request and a response body and reads it back through all three of its endpoints after the clear, because 'the rest still list' is a weaker claim than 'the rest are still whole' and a removal that corrupted the map would satisfy the first. deleting_one_flow_and_the_three_ways_a_delete_is_refused walks 400 (malformed id), 404 (unknown id), 200-with-zero (a filter matching nothing), asserts nothing left the store on any of them, then deletes the real id and asserts the second attempt 404s. a_clear_selects_by_provenance_like_every_other_endpoint exports from one store and imports into another so the ring genuinely holds both kinds, refuses source=nonsense while asserting both rows survive, then clears source=imported and asserts the live capture is the one row left - the delete inheriting ?source= from the shared filter with no delete-specific code, the same construction guarantee the export relies on. a_cursor_a_delete_removed_ends_the_walk_like_an_evicted_one, added by the review: a cursor row can now vanish two ways and paging must answer the same for both, which no test paired before and neither the listing contract nor UC-7 stated. Also extended rather than added: ui_shell_page_and_the_request_sequence_it_performs now asserts the debug shell wires 'cleared' into its refetch list."
  e2e:
    waived: "The epic's own tests/e2e_export.rs step 4 owns this flow end to end - boot the engine, drive real proxied traffic through the proxy at a local test server, subscribe to /stream, then DELETE /flows, asserting the listing empties, the subscriber receives the clear event, and the connection tally is unchanged. A WU-level e2e would boot the same engine on ephemeral ports to assert what that step already asserts against traffic that is real rather than fixture-built, and the app half (a user reaching the clear control) rides the WebDriver suite after W6. Same waiver reasoning W1, W2 and W3 recorded, and the epic gate re-walks it."
note: >-
  E-EXPORT.W4 delivers the ring's third door: DELETE /flows removing what the listing selects,
  DELETE /flows/{id} removing one, and the clear announced on the bus. Three things carry the WU.
  First, removal shares its arithmetic with eviction - which the first shape of this WU CLAIMED but
  did not do, and the review caught. Ring::debit is now the single place a flow is removed from the
  map and its retained bytes subtracted, called from evict's pop-front loop and from unlink, so the
  arithmetic exists once; before the fix, evict kept its own copy while unlink's comment and this
  note both asserted the unification. What is deliberately NOT shared is the order-mending: eviction
  already holds the row it popped, a single removal finds its one row, and only a batch rebuilds the
  order. Routing evict through unlink - the fix both finders implied - was refuted by verification
  as a regression, since it would make every insert O(ring length) and drop the last-row floor. The
  single-id find matters because retained_bytes counts only body bytes, so a capture of body-less
  exchanges grows the row count without limit while the byte cap never fires, and the full rebuild
  it replaced was paid under the lock the live capture path contends on. The two public doors
  (remove_matching over a FlowFilter, remove over a FlowId) are thin over unlink. Second, an id is not a
  listing predicate. FlowFilter gained no id field, because it is the listing rule and adding one
  would make GET /flows?id=... a second, worse detail endpoint; so the id route addresses the ring
  directly while still going through unlink, which is why the byte accounting cannot differ between
  the two doors. Third, FlowsCleared is a new variant rather than a reuse of FlowEvicted, and it
  carries the whole batch. Distinct because a clear is a user acting and an eviction is the ring
  protecting itself - an interface that conflated them would announce 'capture cleared' every time
  memory pressure dropped a row. Batched because clearing a full ring one event at a time would lag
  every subscriber past the broadcast capacity, which is the exact failure sse_lagged exists to
  report, and because a UI needs one list mutation rather than N. Removal deliberately has no
  last-row floor, the one asymmetry with eviction, and that is asserted rather than left as a
  comment. Two things outside the plan: the debug shell (src/api/ui.html) gained 'cleared' in its
  refetch list, because without it a clear followed by silence would leave stale rows on screen
  until the next capture - one word, asserted by the existing shell contract test, and not a
  UI-rendering file under ui/src/ so no design citation is owed. And the CORS widening the plan owed
  had already landed with W3's import: DEC-31 carried the argument and predicted DELETE would join
  the list, so this WU added the method and turned that prediction past tense rather than writing a
  second record making the same argument. Suite green: 169 lib unit, 32 tests/api.rs, 3 tests/ca.rs,
  6 tests/e2e_capture.rs, 4 tests/e2e_h2.rs, 0 failures; cargo fmt --check and clippy -D warnings
  clean.
  Round 2 re-reviewed the fix as unreviewed code and two finders independently found the same
  single defect: the edit that extracted debit had left the old unlink comment above it, so the
  function carried a second docstring naming a parameter it does not take and asserting the design
  the fix had just rejected - the first round's own finding, reintroduced by the edit that fixed
  it. Corrected, with a sweep confirming no other stale claim survives. Two properties of the fix
  were verified rather than argued: the retain predicate inverted (keep-what-the-map-holds to
  drop-what-we-removed) and was proven equivalent by asserting the order/map correspondence inside
  unlink and running the suite, where it holds across the proptests' randomised sequences; and
  doomed can only carry a duplicate if the order does, which freshly minted ids preclude and
  a_repeated_id_is_debited_once guards regardless.
